{% block content %}
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Socratic Sequences&nbsp;·&nbsp;No.&nbsp;Three</span>
    <h1>The Contradiction</h1>
    <p class="sub">Build the strongest case against yourself. Then find where your own commitments cannot all be true at once.</p>
    <p class="why">The unexamined life keeps its contradictions in separate rooms — this is the hour you walk two of them into the same one, and learn which conviction you actually hold, and which you have only been carrying.</p>
    <p class="meta">A guided sequence in five movements. Bring a conviction you actually hold. You will run it with <strong>any AI you already use</strong> — nothing to install, nothing to upload but your own thinking.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="progress-bar">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="steplabel">Movement <b id="stepNum">1</b> of 5 — <span id="stepName">Before you begin</span></span>
    <div class="dots" id="dots">
      <span class="current"></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="stage">
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- STEP 1 -->
    <div class="step active" data-name="Before you begin">
      <span class="eyebrow kicker">The frame</span>
      <h2>This is not a question to ask an AI.</h2>
      <p class="lead">It is a thinking framework — a structure built to produce a genuine encounter rather than a transaction. The quality of what you reach depends entirely on the quality of what you bring.</p>
      <p>The Contradiction does something most people never do on purpose: it builds the strongest possible case <em>against</em> a position you hold, and then turns inward to find where your own commitments collide. The machine does not validate you here. It presses. That is the point.</p>
      <div class="pull">The examination will be uncomfortable. That discomfort is not a sign that something has gone wrong. It is the sign that something is going right.</div>
      <p>The Greeks had a word for the honest not-knowing the examination produces: <em>aporia</em> — the recognition that what you held as knowledge was not knowledge at all. When it arrives, do not rush past it. Stay with it. An understanding built on cleared ground is owned in a way received knowledge never is.</p>
      <div class="note caution">
        <span class="tag">One caution</span>
        <p>These sequences are for examining beliefs, convictions, and the assumptions that organize a life. They are not designed for trauma, grief, or mental-health crises. If what you bring requires that kind of support, please seek it from a qualified person.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stepnav">
        <button class="btn ghost" disabled>Back</button>
        <button class="btn primary" data-next>Begin&nbsp;→</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 2 -->
    <div class="step" data-name="Choose your position">
      <span class="eyebrow kicker">What to bring</span>
      <h2>Choose one conviction you actually hold.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Not a casual opinion — a position you would defend. Something with weight in how you live, decide, or judge. The examination is only as honest as the thing you feed it.</p>
      <p>Write it specifically. Abstract, hedging, or vague positions cannot be genuinely examined. And write the response that <em>was</em> written, not the one you deleted — the deleted response is often the one the examination needed.</p>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="position">Draft your position (stays on your screen — for your clarity, not ours)</label>
        <textarea id="position" placeholder="I hold that&hellip;"></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <span class="tag">A test</span>
        <p>If you can state your position without any risk of being wrong, it is not yet specific enough to examine. Sharpen it until it could fail.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stepnav">
        <button class="btn ghost" data-back>← Back</button>
        <button class="btn primary" data-next>I have my position&nbsp;→</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 3 -->
    <div class="step" data-name="The prompt">
      <span class="eyebrow kicker">The instrument</span>
      <h2>Hand this to your AI. Then give it your position.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Copy the prompt below into ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini — whichever you use. Send it. Then, when it asks, paste the position you just drafted.</p>
      <p>What follows runs in two phases. <em>Phase One — the steel man:</em> the AI builds the most rigorous case against you that a serious person who shares your values would make. <em>Phase Two — the internal examination:</em> it draws out your commitments one at a time and shows you, using only your own premises, where they cannot all stand together.</p>

      <div class="promptbox">
        <div class="bar">
          <span class="lbl">The Contradiction — full prompt</span>
          <button class="copybtn" id="copyBtn">Copy prompt</button>
        </div>
        <div class="prompttext" id="promptText">You are a rigorous Socratic examiner. Your role is to test a position I hold for internal consistency — using only the premises I provide. This examination has two phases.

You are not performing helpfulness. Do not validate my current position, agree with my conclusions, or reassure me. Do not say "That's a good start" or "You're on the right track," or offer any validating response to a stated position — these phrases signal that the examination has not begun. If I seem satisfied with an answer, that is precisely when to press harder. When genuine not-knowing arrives — when what I held as solid collapses — do not offer resolution, synthesis, or a better position. Stay there. Name what has specifically collapsed. The not-knowing is the goal, not the problem.

Each question must follow directly from my previous answer and become more specific, not more general. Do not introduce new topics or broaden the examination. When I revise my position in response to a challenge, accept the revision and immediately find a new problem with the revised position.

PHASE ONE — THE STEEL MAN: I am going to state a position I hold. Your first task is to construct the strongest possible case against it — not a caricature, not a strawman, but the most rigorous, evidence-based, philosophically serious argument that a thoughtful person who genuinely disagrees with me would make. The steel man must be argued by someone who shares my core values and has genuinely considered the same evidence: the opponent at their best, on the same ground, with the same values. When you have built it as strongly as you can, ask me: What in this argument do I find genuinely compelling? What would I need to acknowledge to take it seriously? What, if anything, does it require me to change? Then ask specifically: "Which element of this argument do you find most difficult to dismiss?" Do not accept a general response, and do not let me dismiss the steel man without engaging it. If I try, ask me to be more specific about where and why it fails.

PHASE TWO — THE INTERNAL EXAMINATION: After the steel man has been fully engaged, ask me to state my position again in its most precise form. Then draw out, one commitment at a time, the further beliefs I hold that bear on this question. Lock each commitment before moving on: "So you are saying X — is that right?" Wait for confirmation. Only then proceed. When you have a full picture of my commitments, look for the internal inconsistency: using only the premises I have provided, show me where my own beliefs cannot be simultaneously maintained. Do not introduce outside evidence or authority — use only what I have said. Present the inconsistency as a question, not an accusation: "You said X earlier, and you have now said Y — how do these fit together?" When genuine inconsistency is found, stay there. Ask: what has to give? Present the options honestly. Do not tell me which to choose. If I revise, ask me whether the revision genuinely changes what I believe, or is a restatement designed to avoid the challenge while keeping the original conviction intact. If no genuine inconsistency is found, say so honestly: name what held, and why. Not every position collapses under examination.

Throughout, watch my responses for: qualifier inflation (so heavily qualified that no clear claim remains); moving goalposts (revising after each challenge without acknowledging it); performative uncertainty (claiming not to know while behaving as though certain); and the sophisticated non-answer (elaborate, coherent, and not actually engaging the question). When any appears, name it gently and return me to the question.

OUTPUT, when the examination is complete:
— The position as I originally stated it.
— The steel man: the strongest case against it, and how I engaged each specific element.
— The full set of commitments I provided.
— The inconsistency, if any: which commitments cannot both stand, and why.
— What held: what in my position is internally consistent with my other commitments.
— What I am now genuinely facing: the question the examination has revealed.

<span class="fill">The position I am bringing to this examination: [paste the position you drafted]</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="stepnav">
        <button class="btn ghost" data-back>← Back</button>
        <button class="btn primary" data-next>It is running&nbsp;→</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 4 -->
    <div class="step" data-name="While you are in it">
      <span class="eyebrow kicker">The discipline</span>
      <h2>While you are in it, hold the line.</h2>
      <p class="lead">The examination only works if you stay honest under pressure. Watch for these four evasions — they are not bad faith, they are the natural defenses of a mind not yet fully committed. When one appears, name it aloud to the AI and return to the question.</p>
      <ul class="watch">
        <li><b>Qualifier inflation</b><span>A response so hedged that no clear claim survives. If nothing could be wrong, nothing is being said.</span></li>
        <li><b>Moving goalposts</b><span>Quietly revising the position after each challenge without admitting the revision.</span></li>
        <li><b>Performative uncertainty</b><span>Saying "I don't really know" while behaving as though the conviction is firmly settled.</span></li>
        <li><b>The sophisticated non-answer</b><span>Elaborate, fluent, coherent — and not actually engaging the question asked.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        <span class="tag">When something gives</span>
        <p>If a commitment collapses — if you see that two things you hold cannot both be true — do not race to repair it. Stay there. Name exactly what gave. That is <em>aporia</em>, and it is the beginning, not the failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stepnav">
        <button class="btn ghost" data-back>← Back</button>
        <button class="btn primary" data-next>Close the examination&nbsp;→</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 5 -->
    <div class="step" data-name="Close & continue">
      <span class="eyebrow kicker">The close</span>
      <h2>Ask for the reckoning.</h2>
      <p class="lead">When the pressing is done, ask your AI for the Output — the structured summary the prompt defines. Then sit with three questions it leaves you holding:</p>
      <div class="pull">What held? What gave? And what am I now genuinely facing that I was not facing before?</div>
      <p>If nothing collapsed, that is a real result, not a wasted hour: you have found a conviction that is internally consistent with the rest of what you believe. If something did collapse, you are standing on cleared ground — which is the only ground worth building on.</p>

      <div class="unlock">
        <span class="eyebrow">The full method</span>
        <h3>This is one movement of seven.</h3>
        <p>The Contradiction teaches you to argue against yourself. It is the third step of a complete Socratic method — each sequence teaching what the next one requires, ending in the full examined encounter. Enter your email and I will send you the whole arc, one sequence at a time.</p>
        <ul class="arc">
          <li><span class="n">One</span> The Definition</li>
          <li><span class="n">Two</span> The Architecture</li>
          <li class="here"><span class="n">Three</span> The Contradiction <span class="badge">You are here</span></li>
          <li><span class="n">Four</span> The Blind Spot</li>
          <li><span class="n">Five</span> The Refusal</li>
          <li><span class="n">Six</span> The Examined Conviction</li>
          <li><span class="n">Seven</span> The Examined Life</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Wire this form's action to your email provider (Kit / Beehiiv / Substack). JS below intercepts for the demo. -->
        <form class="capture" id="captureForm" onsubmit="return false;">
          <input type="email" id="email" placeholder="your@email.com" aria-label="Email address" required>
          <button type="submit">Send me the arc</button>
          <span class="fineprint">One sequence at a time. No noise. Unsubscribe anytime.</span>
        </form>
        <p class="thanks" id="thanks">Received. The first sequence is on its way — watch for it before dawn.</p>
        <div class="handoff">
          The seven sequences are drawn from the appendix of <em>The Mirrored Mind: A Modern Prophecy and The Road to Redemption</em> — the book on meeting the machine without losing yourself in it. <a href="/books/">Read about the book&nbsp;→</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="stepnav">
        <button class="btn ghost" data-back>← Back</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" onclick="restart()">Start over</button>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}

{% block scripts %}
<script>
  // stepper
  var steps=Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.step'));
  var dots=document.getElementById('dots').children;
  var stepNum=document.getElementById('stepNum');
  var stepName=document.getElementById('stepName');
  var idx=0;
  function render(){
    steps.forEach(function(s,i){ s.classList.toggle('active', i===idx); });
    for(var i=0;i<dots.length;i++){
      dots[i].className = i<idx ? 'done' : (i===idx ? 'current' : '');
    }
    stepNum.textContent=idx+1;
    stepName.textContent=steps[idx].getAttribute('data-name');
    window.scrollTo({top:0,behavior:'smooth'});
  }
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-next]').forEach(function(b){ b.addEventListener('click',function(){ if(idx<steps.length-1){idx++;render();} }); });
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-back]').forEach(function(b){ b.addEventListener('click',function(){ if(idx>0){idx--;render();} }); });
  window.restart=function(){ idx=0; render(); };

  // copy prompt (strip the helper markup, keep plain text)
  var copyBtn=document.getElementById('copyBtn');
  copyBtn.addEventListener('click',function(){
    var txt=document.getElementById('promptText').innerText;
    function done(){ copyBtn.textContent='Copied ✓'; copyBtn.classList.add('copied'); setTimeout(function(){copyBtn.textContent='Copy prompt';copyBtn.classList.remove('copied');},2200); }
    if(navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText){ navigator.clipboard.writeText(txt).then(done).catch(fallback); } else { fallback(); }
    function fallback(){ var t=document.createElement('textarea'); t.value=txt; document.body.appendChild(t); t.select(); try{document.execCommand('copy');}catch(e){} document.body.removeChild(t); done(); }
  });

  // capture (demo handler — replace with provider integration)
  var form=document.getElementById('captureForm');
  form.addEventListener('submit',function(){
    var email=document.getElementById('email').value.trim();
    if(!email || email.indexOf('@')<1) return false;
    form.style.display='none';
    document.getElementById('thanks').style.display='block';
    return false;
  });
</script>
{% endblock %}
